{
  // tsconfig.cal-test.json — the booking module's bun:test suite.
  //
  // BUN GLOBALS, since 2026-09-02, and that is a fact about how these tests
  // RUN. The suite executes on the host: route handlers, the booking store and
  // the email builders are imported straight from cal/src into bun's process,
  // and only the bindings live in the workerd that wrangler's createTestHarness
  // boots (cal/test/harness.ts). So `fetch`, `Response` and `crypto` here are
  // bun's, `env.BOOKINGS` is a proxy, and the program declares bun's globals,
  // the same entry tsconfig.cf-garage-test.json and tsconfig.tools.json use.
  //
  // The consequence is the one those two headers already describe: importing
  // the Worker pulls cal/src in against the wrong scope, so a Workers-only
  // shape reads as a diagnostic here and is clean in tsconfig.json next door.
  // That is a missing declaration rather than a finding, which is why this
  // program is NOT run bare. `bun run typecheck:tests` filters to cal/test/ and
  // asserts the program holds every test file on disk, the floor that keeps
  // the filter from quietly scanning nothing.
  //
  // Until 2026-09-02 this was a Workers-globals program run as a plain `tsc -p`
  // from the `typecheck` script, because @cloudflare/vitest-pool-workers ran
  // the suite inside workerd and the globals matched. That era is why its
  // header used to argue against a wrapper; the runtime moved and the argument
  // moved with it.
  //
  // The suite was in no program at all until 2026-08-23. It found 8 real
  // diagnostics on its first run, all one cause: booking fixtures built as
  // `{start, end, status, created}` against an `Omit<Booking, "id">` parameter
  // that requires name, email and topic. The tests passed throughout, because a
  // missing property is only visible to a checker.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "target": "esnext",
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    // cal/src/templates.js imports the site Worker by its real `.ts` filename,
    // for the reason tsconfig.json's header gives: that specifier is the one
    // that resolves in both trees. Without this the suite's program rejects it
    // and never reaches a finding.
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "lib": ["esnext"],
    // bun-types carries node's globals, which is what these tests run on.
    // The Workers runtime declarations are ALSO in this program, through the
    // generated include below, and that is the one deliberate union in
    // config/: harness.ts's whole job is to type workerd bindings (KVNamespace,
    // Workflow, ExecutionContext) from a bun host, and the alternative, a
    // type-only import from @cloudflare/workers-types, left with that package
    // on 2026-09-02. The filter in check-test-types still reports only
    // cal/test's own diagnostics, so the cost is narrow: a Workers-only call
    // written in a TEST file would pass here. cal/src is checked against the
    // Workers globals alone next door.
    "types": ["bun"],
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    // Two control-flow checks, set here because they are set in tsconfig.json
    // and a program is only as strict as its own options. Both cost nothing:
    // +0 diagnostics on every one of the ten programs, measured 2026-08-27.
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,
    // strictNullChecks ON. `strict` stays off: it bundles noImplicitAny,
    // which is 7,197 diagnostics of annotation labour across this repo, and
    // raising the two together buries the checks that find bugs under the
    // ones that find missing annotations. This program is clean under it,
    // and a contract test keeps the set of clean programs from shrinking.
    "strictNullChecks": true,
    "strict": false
  },
  // harness.ts and preload.ts are here deliberately. They decide which runtime
  // the suite gets and what it can import, so a program covering the tests and
  // skipping them covers the tests and not the thing that makes them tests.
  "include": ["../cal/test/**/*.js", "../cal/test/**/*.ts", "./.generated/workers-runtime.d.ts"]
}
